  "worked_solution": "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
